{"audio":{"src":"https://pub-13abeb101e2945d2a7f60234f6faf52e.r2.dev/materials/b1-explainer-about-lithuanian-history-from-the-first-mention-of-the-name-through-today-gat/audio/main-29a2d3e55fb2.mp3","durationSec":280.32,"cues":[{"start":0,"end":6.22,"text":"The earliest known written record of Lithuania’s name comes from the Annals of Quedlinburg."},{"start":6.22,"end":11.4,"text":"It described events from 9 March 1009 and used the Latin form *Litua*."},{"start":12.34,"end":15.48,"text":"However, this record was not the beginning of the Lithuanian people."},{"start":16.2,"end":18.94,"text":"It was simply the first surviving written mention."},{"start":20.14,"end":22.76,"text":"The exact meaning of *Lietuva* is still debated."},{"start":23.68,"end":28.24,"text":"One theory connects it to the small Lietava River near Kernavė."},{"start":28.24,"end":32.4,"text":"Another connects it to the *leičiai*, a historical warrior group."},{"start":34.2,"end":37.3,"text":"Lithuanians are Balts, not Slavs."},{"start":37.58,"end":45.1,"text":"Lithuanian is an East Baltic language related to Latvian, although speakers of the two languages cannot automatically understand each other."},{"start":46.18,"end":50.5,"text":"It preserves some very old features of the Indo-European language family."},{"start":51.2,"end":56.34,"text":"In the 13th century, Baltic tribes in Aukštaitija formed a pagan state."},{"start":56.88,"end":64.6,"text":"Mindaugas was crowned the Catholic King of Lithuania on 6 July 1253, although he later returned to pagan practice."},{"start":65.92,"end":69.18,"text":"The Grand Duchy of Lithuania developed from this early state."},{"start":70.1,"end":74.3,"text":"Gediminas made Vilnius the lasting capital from 1323."},{"start":75.54,"end":80.6,"text":"During the 14th and 15th centuries, the Grand Duchy became one of Europe’s largest states."},{"start":81.4,"end":90.94,"text":"Around 1430, it stretched from the Baltic Sea toward the Black Sea and covered about 930,000 square kilometres."},{"start":90.94,"end":93.72,"text":"Lithuania was the last major pagan state in Europe."},{"start":94.56,"end":101.24,"text":"After Jogaila signed the Union of Krewo in 1385, Lithuania was Christianized in 1387."},{"start":102.12,"end":107.16,"text":"In 1410, Polish–Lithuanian forces defeated the Teutonic Knights at Grunwald."},{"start":107.58,"end":112.66,"text":"The Union of Lublin created the Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth in 1569."},{"start":113.5,"end":118.6,"text":"Lithuania shared a state with Poland but kept its own laws, army, and treasury."},{"start":119.42,"end":127.84,"text":"After three partitions, the Third Partition ended the state in 1795, and the Russian Empire took most Lithuanian lands, including Vilnius."},{"start":128.7,"end":129.82,"text":"Resistance continued."},{"start":130.5,"end":137.2,"text":"Lithuanians joined uprisings in 1830–1831 and 1863–1864."},{"start":138.1,"end":142.92,"text":"Russia banned Lithuanian books printed in the Latin alphabet from 1864."},{"start":143.74,"end":149.04,"text":"Book smugglers, called *knygnešiai*, secretly carried such books from East Prussia."},{"start":150.02,"end":157.24,"text":"On 16 February 1918, twenty members of the Council of Lithuania signed the Act of Independence in Vilnius."},{"start":157.66,"end":162.9,"text":"The Act restored an independent democratic Lithuanian state with Vilnius as its capital."},{"start":163.88,"end":169.72,"text":"In August 1939, the Molotov–Ribbentrop Pact placed the Baltic states in the Soviet sphere."},{"start":170.62,"end":173.88,"text":"The Soviet Union occupied Lithuania in June 1940."},{"start":174.74,"end":180.08,"text":"Nazi Germany occupied it in 1941, and Soviet forces returned in 1944."},{"start":180.08,"end":189.2,"text":"Anti-Soviet partisans continued fighting into the early 1950s, while about 130,000 Lithuanians were deported to Siberia."},{"start":190.18,"end":194.5,"text":"On 11 March 1990, Lithuania declared the restoration of independence."},{"start":195.24,"end":202.4,"text":"“Restoration” was important: the Act stated that the independence declared in 1918 had never legally lost its force."},{"start":202.4,"end":205.3,"text":"Moscow responded with an economic blockade."},{"start":205.82,"end":213.3,"text":"On 13 January 1991, Soviet forces attacked important buildings in Vilnius, killing fourteen unarmed people."},{"start":214.32,"end":221.86,"text":"The Soviet Union recognized Lithuania on 6 September 1991, and the last Russian army units left in 1993."},{"start":222.68,"end":228.52,"text":"Modern Lithuania is a country of about 2.9 million people on the eastern Baltic Sea."},{"start":229.56,"end":235.5,"text":"Its capital is Vilnius, Lithuanian is its official language, and Klaipėda is its main port."},{"start":236.68,"end":240.8,"text":"Forests cover about one third of the land, and there are more than 3,000 lakes."},{"start":241.96,"end":246.58,"text":"Lithuania joined NATO and the European Union in 2004."},{"start":246.58,"end":249.96,"text":"It entered the eurozone in 2015, so it uses the euro."},{"start":250.82,"end":256.1,"text":"These memberships connect the restored state closely with European and transatlantic institutions."},{"start":256.1,"end":260.32,"text":"Older history remains visible in modern culture."},{"start":260.96,"end":266.32,"text":"Baltic amber was once traded to the Roman Empire, and Palanga now has an amber museum."},{"start":267.3,"end":272.54,"text":"The Hill of Crosses near Šiauliai became a symbol of Catholic resistance during the Soviet period."},{"start":273.64,"end":279.66,"text":"Basketball is also a major part of modern culture; Lithuania hosted EuroBasket in 2011."}],"words":[{"text":"The","start":0,"end":0.16},{"text":"earliest","start":0.16,"end":0.58},{"text":"known","start":0.58,"end":0.98},{"text":"written","start":0.98,"end":1.24},{"text":"record","start":1.24,"end":1.64},{"text":"of","start":1.64,"end":1.94},{"text":"Lithuania’s","start":1.94,"end"